import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var o={photoelectric:{theory:{concept:"[[The photoelectric effect is the emission of electrons from a metal when light falls on it.]] Light arrives in small packets of energy \u2014 photons \u2014 each carrying E = h\xB7f, and one photon gives all its energy to one electron. To escape, the electron needs at least the work function \u03C6 of the metal; the rest becomes its kinetic energy: K_max = h\xB7f \u2212 \u03C6. So whether electrons come out depends on the frequency (colour) of the light, not on its brightness: brighter light only releases more electrons."},section:{photons:{title:"Light comes in photons",text:"Light behaves like a stream of photons. The energy of one photon depends only on the frequency: E = h\xB7f, where h = 6.63\xB710\u207B\xB3\u2074 J\xB7s is Planck's constant. Violet light (400 nm) has photons of 3.1 eV, red light (650 nm) only 1.9 eV, ultraviolet even more. A brighter lamp sends more photons per second, but each photon has the same energy."},effect:{title:"What happens in the tube",text:"The lamp shines on a metal plate (the cathode) in a vacuum tube. When a photon is absorbed, it can free an electron. The freed electrons fly to the second plate (the anode) and the ammeter shows a current. In the simulation each dot stands for very many photons or electrons, and everything moves enormously slowed down."},threshold:{title:"A threshold that brightness cannot beat",text:"[[Every metal holds its electrons with a certain energy: the work function \u03C6 (2.3 eV for potassium, 4.3 eV for zinc).]] If a photon has less energy than \u03C6, no electron comes out \u2014 however bright the light and however long you wait. The longest wavelength that still works is the threshold \u03BB\u2080 = h\xB7c / \u03C6 (539 nm for potassium). Above the threshold the fastest electrons have K_max = h\xB7f \u2212 \u03C6."},brightness:{title:"Brightness and colour do different things",text:"[[Make the lamp brighter: more photons arrive, more electrons come out and the current grows \u2014 but the electrons are not faster.]] Change the colour towards violet or ultraviolet: each electron gets more energy and flies faster. A classical wave picture of light predicted the opposite (brighter light should shake electrons harder), which is why this experiment was so important."},stopping:{title:"Measuring the energy with a voltage",text:"Turn the battery around so the anode is negative (U < 0): the field now pushes the electrons back. Slow electrons turn around before they reach the anode, and the current falls. At the stopping voltage U\u2080 even the fastest electrons are stopped: e\xB7U\u2080 = K_max. So a voltmeter measures the electrons' energy in electronvolts. With U > 0 every freed electron is collected, and the current reaches its saturation value."},einstein:{title:"Hertz, Einstein and Millikan",text:"Heinrich Hertz noticed in 1887 that ultraviolet light helps sparks jump. In 1902 Philipp Lenard found that the electrons' energy does not depend on the brightness. In 1905 Albert Einstein explained this with light quanta \u2014 he received the 1921 Nobel Prize for it. Robert Millikan did not believe the idea, tested it carefully for ten years and in 1916 confirmed it: K_max grows in a straight line with f, and the slope is Planck's constant h."},today:{title:"Photons at work today",text:"Camera sensors in phones, solar cells and light sensors use the related inner photoelectric effect in semiconductors, where light frees electrons inside the material. Photomultipliers and night-vision devices use the outer effect shown here and can detect single photons. Modern experiments in quantum physics and quantum cryptography count photons one by one."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"Red light shines on potassium and nothing happens. Will a brighter red lamp help? (No \u2014 each photon still has too little energy.) What changes if you use a brighter violet lamp? (More current, same electron energy.) The stopping voltage is 1.5 V. What is K_max? (1.5 eV.) Which metal needs ultraviolet light: potassium or zinc? (Zinc \u2014 its work function is larger.) Try each one in the simulation."}},formula:{photon:"Energy of one photon (h: Planck's constant)",einstein:"Einstein's equation: photon energy = work function + largest kinetic energy",threshold:"Threshold wavelength: longer waves free no electrons",stopping:"The stopping voltage measures the largest kinetic energy",photonRate:"Photons per second for light power P",current:"Saturation current (\u03B7: electrons per photon)",speed:"Speed of the fastest electrons"},variable:{planck:"Planck's constant, 6.63\xB710\u207B\xB3\u2074 J\xB7s = 4.14\xB710\u207B\xB9\u2075 eV\xB7s",efficiency:"Quantum efficiency (electrons per photon)"},assumption:{workFunction:"Typical work functions of clean metals; the real value depends on the surface and on impurities.",spread:"The freed electrons' kinetic energies are spread evenly between 0 and K_max (in a real metal the spread depends on the electrons' starting energies).",efficiency:"The quantum efficiency is 0.1 % for every metal and wavelength, and all light that reaches the cathode is counted (up to 10 \xB5W).",dots:"Each drawn photon or electron stands for very many, and the motion is slowed down enormously.",tube:"An ideal vacuum tube: electrons fly straight across, no space charge, no contact voltage between the plates, and the anode emits nothing. Not to scale."},param:{wavelength:"Wavelength (colour) of the light",brightness:"Brightness of the lamp",metal:"Cathode metal",voltage:"Battery voltage (anode \u2212 cathode)",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{wavelength:"Shorter wavelength = higher frequency = more energy per photon. Below 380 nm is invisible ultraviolet, above 750 nm infrared.",brightness:"How much light power reaches the plate (100 % = 10 \xB5W): how many photons per second.",metal:"Each metal holds its electrons differently: its work function.",voltage:"Positive: the anode attracts the electrons. Negative: it pushes them back."},preset:{uv:"UV 250 nm",violet:"Violet 400 nm",blue:"Blue 450 nm",green:"Green 530 nm",red:"Red 650 nm",brake:"\u22122 V",zero:"0 V",speedUp:"+5 V"},metal:{cesium:"Cesium (\u03C6 = 2.14 eV)",potassium:"Potassium (\u03C6 = 2.30 eV)",zinc:"Zinc (\u03C6 = 4.33 eV)",copper:"Copper (\u03C6 = 4.65 eV)",platinum:"Platinum (\u03C6 = 5.64 eV)"},measure:{photonEnergy:"Energy of one photon",maxKineticEnergy:"Largest kinetic energy of an electron",current:"Photocurrent",workFunction:"Work function",threshold:"Threshold wavelength",stoppingVoltage:"Stopping voltage",frequency:"Frequency of the light",photonRate:"Photons per second on the plate",saturation:"Saturation current",collected:"Share of electrons reaching the anode",maxSpeed:"Speed of the fastest electrons"},graph:{currentVoltage:"Current vs voltage",energyFrequency:"Largest electron energy vs frequency"},view:{lamp:"lamp \xB7 {wavelength} \xB7 {colour}",band:{uv:"ultraviolet (invisible)",violet:"violet",blue:"blue",cyan:"cyan",green:"green",yellow:"yellow",orange:"orange",red:"red",ir:"infrared (invisible)"},cathode:"cathode: {metal}",anode:"anode",vacuum:"vacuum",legend:"small dots: photons \xB7 blue dots: electrons (\u2212)",forceForward:"force on electrons \u2192 anode",forceBack:"force on electrons \u2190 back",balance:"hf = {photon}, \u03C6 = {phi} \u2192 K_max = {kmax}",tooWeak:"hf = {photon} < \u03C6 = {phi}: no electrons",photonsOption:"Photons",fieldOption:"Force on electrons"},challenge:{nothing:{title:"Bright, but nothing happens",description:"Turn the lamp to at least 90 % brightness so that still no electrons come out.",hint:"Brightness does not help if each photon has too little energy. Try a longer wavelength."},current:{title:"More current",description:"Make a photocurrent of at least 3 nA.",hint:"More photons per second free more electrons. At the same power, longer waves (still above the threshold) mean more photons."},fast:{title:"Fast electrons",description:"Give the fastest electrons at least 2 eV of kinetic energy.",hint:"K_max = h\xB7f \u2212 \u03C6: use photons with more energy."},stop:{title:"Stop them all",description:"Electrons are being freed, but make the current zero.",hint:"Make the anode negative until even the fastest electrons turn back: e\xB7U \u2264 \u2212K_max."},threshold:{title:"Zinc's threshold",description:"Choose zinc and find a wavelength just short enough to free electrons (K_max at most 0.05 eV).",hint:"\u03BB\u2080 = h\xB7c / \u03C6 = 1240 eV\xB7nm / 4.33 eV."},stopping:{title:"1.5 volts",description:"Set up an experiment whose stopping voltage is 1.5 V (\xB1 0.02 V).",hint:"U\u2080 = (h\xB7f \u2212 \u03C6)/e, so you need photons of \u03C6 + 1.5 eV."},half:{title:"Half the current",description:"With a braking (negative) voltage, let exactly half of the freed electrons reach the anode (\xB1 1 %).",hint:"The energies are spread evenly from 0 to K_max. Which voltage stops the slower half?"}}}},n={sim:o};export{n as default,o as sim};
